# ============================================================
# Filename: .dockerignore
# Author: Professor Denis O. Núñez
# Email: donunez@programmingjourneys.com
# Organization: Programming Journeys
# Project: Project 09 - Korra: MCP Server and User Interface
# Module: Module 09 - Interfacing and Communication
# Layer: Deployment - Container
# Date Created: October 4, 2026
# Date Modified: October 4, 2026
# Version: M09S04
# License: Educational Use
#
# Description:
#     Keeps the backend build context small and keeps Windows build output
#     out of a Linux image. Copy into the korra folder as ".dockerignore"
#     with the leading dot.
#
#     Two kinds of pattern below, and the difference matters. Docker matches
#     a pattern against the whole path and a * does not cross a directory
#     separator, so a bare name matches only at the top of the context. The
#     **/ prefix is what reaches further down.
#
#     That is why *.exe alone is not enough: the Windows binary sits five
#     folders deep, the wrapper looks for that name before the Linux one,
#     and an image built without this rule finds a binary it cannot run.
#
#     The template leftovers are listed without **/ on purpose, so the
#     tools folder keeps its own Makefile and tests, which the build needs
#     to compile the C tool and run its checks.
# ============================================================

# Windows and local build output, at any depth
**/*.exe
**/*.o
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.egg-info

# Keys arrive through env_file at run time, never baked into the image
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/file_stats
**/test_file_stats
.venv

# Local dev-server state, meaningless inside a container
.langgraph_api

# Template leftovers from langgraph new. Top level only: src/react_agent/
# tools keeps its own Makefile and tests
.git
.github
.gitignore
.codespellignore
LICENSE
Makefile
README.md
static
tests
uv.lock
